Z||000000||samt601||03-10-2001||01:15 AM||||I have a 1984 fiero se 4cyl and the motor idle revs from 500 rpm to 2000 rpm while warming up and once it is warmed up the reving calms down a bit.<BR>I am fairly this is caused by sensor(s) but there are so many sensors i dont know where to start.||||reg||8
Z||000001||deceler8||03-10-2001||10:45 AM||||Run your codes and repost. If you're not sure how to do this, use the search function.<P>My guess is bad idle air control valve and/or some large vacuum leak(s).||||reg||1
Z||000002||KRMFiero||03-10-2001||12:08 PM||||My 85 does same thing, i dont think its has went down to 500rpm yet....  its about 2-3k rpm  and when it warms up it goes to about 1100 rpm, i ordered a trottle cabl it came yesturday, i dont know if that had to do with it but my cable is melted in some areas and stuff so it needs to be replaced.<P>Kyle<P>------------------<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE (Red)<BR>1985 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE (White)<P>     <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/~Physcokyle/sig2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||MontanaFiero||03-10-2001||03:46 PM||||I agree with deceler8<B> (who now has 666 posts).</B>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>I think it might be the IAC, because those wear out over time. The air rushing around the cone wears the cone down so it won't seat right. Dirt may also be a factor. Follow your Chilton manual on how to replace it, just be careful. Don't re-install it unless you've read the "how-to" or you'll surely mess it up.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/blee291/fiero/mtfieroplate.jpg">   <BR>&gt;&gt; '87 Coupe 4 cyl, auto &lt;&lt;<BR>&gt;&gt; '86 SE V6, 4 speed- <I>project</I> &lt;&lt;||||reg||1
Z||000004||Eric_PA||03-11-2001||07:39 AM||||I just fixed my 84 last night with the same problem. It was the IAC for me, would you beleive I took the IAC off of my parents 92 Blazer and it worked  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> How nice of me to go to there place use there garage and then take parts from there truck. I did replace it. I found that the Fiero IAC was all gumed up with dirt and indeed was worn out. It fixed my 3500 rpm idle. I got a lot accomplished last night with this little beast  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Eric ||||reg||1
